Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 02:43:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(linux): add a guest memory safety net on arm64

On Windows a vectored exception handler (NullPageHandler) lets the game
survive the bad memory accesses the original console code makes: near
null and freed guest memory reads give zeros, writes are dropped, calls
through null pointers return. Linux had nothing, so the first such
access ended the game.

guest_safety_net_posix.cpp does the same on aarch64 Linux through the
SDK's exception handler:

- the low 16 MB of guest memory is committed and the null object page
  zeroed, as on Windows; a fault there that comes back re-commits the
  page a few times, then the access is skipped;
- other guest pages are committed on demand, except the GPU registers
  (MMIO), which stay with the SDK;
- a load or store the net cannot fix is decoded and skipped: loads give
  zeros (single registers, pairs, vector structures and lanes, exclusive
  loads, compare and swap and the LSE atomics), the base register moves
  on for pre and post index forms;
- a call to a null address or into the null object page returns 0 (1
  after the same call failed 20 times in a row, to end wait loops);
- brk traps (__builtin_trap) are skipped;
- a fault it still cannot fix ends that worker thread; on the main
  thread the process ends with the original signal instead of faulting
  forever.
